    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000123537,
      "SummaryText": "Flowserve Corporation Kingman Risk Management Plan Executive Summary \nThe accidental release prevention and emergency response policies at the stationary source: Flowserve Corporation Kingman (Flowserve) has implemented a Risk Management Program in accordance with the requirements of the Chemical Accident Prevention Provisions. In the event of an emergency, Flowserve Corporation Kingman has coordinated emergency response efforts with the Mohave Country Local Emergency Planning Committee (LEPC). \n \nThe stationary source and regulated substances handled: Flowserve supports and tests cryogenic liquified natural gas (LNG) pumps. At the time of this submittal, Flowserve is subject to the requirements under the Chemical Accident Prevention Provisions due to the onsite storage and usage of LNG in a quantity greater than the 10,000-pound threshold. \n \nThe worst-case release scenario and the alternative release scenario, including administrative controls and mitigation measures to limit the distances for each reported scenario: Flowserve has administrative controls (procedures) which limit the amount of LNG which can be stored onsite. Flowserve utilizes relief valves, rupture disks, an enclosed flare, automatic shutoffs, interlocks, and alarms to protect against identified fire, explosion, and overpressurization hazards. Flowserve also maintains a deluge system and utilizes process area detectors. The worst-case release scenario at the facility is based on the accidental release of the greatest amount held in a single vessel, as specified in the regulation. Therefore, the worst-case scenario is assumed to be the catastrophic failure of the 15,000-gallon LNG storage tank and subsequent vapor cloud explosion. According to the calculations performed using EPA's guidance, the distance to the defined overpressure endpoint of 1 PSI is 0.3 miles. The alternative release scenario is assumed to be a vessel leak due to failure of valve packing and subsequent vapor cloud fire. For cons"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000123537,
      "SummaryText": "ervatism, this calculation assumed the contents of the leak to be 100% methane. According to calculations performed using EPA's guidance, the distance to the lower flammability limit of methane (33.0 mg/L) is 0.1 miles for the alternative release scenario. \n \nThe general accidental release prevention program and chemical-specific prevention steps: To meet the requirements of the Chemical Accident Prevention Provisions, Flowserve has implemented the Risk Management Program 3 requirements. \n \nThe 5-Year accident history: This is a new site; therefore, the requirement to provide a five-year history of accidental releases of LNG that caused off-site deaths, injuries, or response or restoration activities at environmental receptors does not apply for this submittal. \n \nThe emergency response program: Flowserve has developed an emergency response program in accordance with the requirements of the Chemical Accident Prevention Provisions. Additionally, Flowserve has coordinated emergency response with the Mohave County LEPC. In the event of an emergency, Flowserve will follow the procedures outlined in the written emergency response plan."
    }
  ]
